# class A { m() { this.x } }  class B { m() { this.x } }  class C { m() { this.x } }  function run(o) { o.m() }  run(new A()); run(new B()); run(new C())
# as-many-receivers-as-are-told-apart-reach-their-own-methods.ir with one receiver more than the analysis tells apart. The loss is that one receiver: the first two are told apart as before,
